  "narrative": {
    "intro": "At and T Enterprises reported $689M in contract obligations for FY2026 to date. At and T Enterprises received 175 awards so far, with an average award size of $3.9M. At and T Enterprises obligations are down 33.6% year over year. At and T Enterprises saw its largest agency customer in FY2026 to date as the Department of Veterans Affairs at $248M, followed by the Department of Justice at $149M and the Department of Homeland Security at $129M. At and T Enterprises' work is concentrated in telecommunications markets, with Wired Telecommunications Carriers at $685M and smaller amounts in All Other Telecommunications at $2M and Other Computer Related Services at $724K. At and T Enterprises fiscal-year obligations rose and fell across recent years, from $167M in FY21 to $1B in FY25, then to $689M in FY26 to date. This snapshot is fiscal year to date and reflects contract obligations rather than total contract values for the largest-awards and expiring-contracts tables.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are At and T Enterprises' obligations?",
        "body": "At and T Enterprises' obligations are highly concentrated in telecommunications, with Wired Telecommunications Carriers accounting for $685M of FY2026 to date obligations. At and T Enterprises' top three agency customers together account for a majority of FY2026 to date obligations, led by the Department of Veterans Affairs at $248M, the Department of Justice at $149M, and the Department of Homeland Security at $129M. At and T Enterprises average award size is $3.9M across 175 awards, which signals relatively large awards on average. At and T Enterprises obligations are down 33.6% year over year."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies drive At and T Enterprises' FY2026 to date revenue?",
        "body": "Department of Veterans Affairs is the largest buyer at $248M in FY2026 to date. Department of Justice is the second-largest buyer at $149M, followed by Department of Homeland Security at $129M. Department of Transportation at $42.1M and Department of the Treasury at $32.8M round out the next largest buyers in FY2026 to date."
      }
    ]
  },
